#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048245 :topic/difficulty 1.2094931906633881 :topic/encompasses [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186429226 :encompassing/rationale "Initial Codex estimate from captured target-band crossing frequency. Selected account foundations: 1 rises / 2 eligible passed source activities = 0.500000; nearest selected fraction = 1/2. Crossing activity numbers [2]; waits [2]. Separate account estimates: foundations=1/2 (1/2). Selection: most observations, then lower weight, then account name; no averaging. Single-activity estimates included. Intervening target updates: False. Crossing frequency is the chosen practical estimate of encompassing coverage; a multi-band rise counts once. This does not interpret color steps as physical repetition units. Method: encompassing-report.md. Evidence: reference/mathacademy/encompassing-analysis-2026-10-11/frequency-fractions/foundations-frequency-estimates.json, pair 3770->1361. All account evidence retained in the prepared import's selected-relationships.json." :encompassing/topic {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049443} :encompassing/weight 0.5}] :topic/id #uuid "8bdb85a0-8774-5298-bc46-db4f7969ae17" :topic/knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184349 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186183945 :question/id #uuid "3e9586ee-12d1-5dbb-81f6-0f0a4998e00a" :question/math-academy-id "e-14402" :question/problem "Calculate  $$\n\\displaystyle{\\int x^2 e^{x^3+1}\\, \\textrm{d}x }.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let's introduce the new variable $u = x^3+1.$ Differentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}u}{\\textrm{d}x}= 3x^2\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad\\dfrac 1 3 \\textrm{d}u = x^2 \\, \\textrm{d}x.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {x}^{2}{e}^{{x}^{3}+1}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int {e}^{{x}^{3}+1}\\cdot \\,{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int {e}^{u}\\cdot \\frac{1}{3}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\int {e}^{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{e}^{u}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{e}^{{x}^{3}+1}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e803bd99-388f-5146-ab91-03fd7b658c2c" :knowledge-point/title "Integrating Products Containing the Exponential Function"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184350 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186183946 :question/id #uuid "ab4772a2-f980-5ad7-a377-34e34b95be83" :question/math-academy-id "e-14403" :question/problem "Calculate  $$\n\\displaystyle \\int x^3\\,{3^{1-x^4}} \\, \\textrm{d}x.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let's introduce the new variable $u= 1-x^4.$ Differentiating gives \n\n$$\n\\dfrac{ \\textrm{d}u}{ \\textrm{d}x} = -4x^3\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad x^3 \\, \\textrm{d}x = -\\dfrac{1}{4} \\textrm{d}u.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\int {x^3\\,{3^{1-x^4}} }\\, \\textrm{d}x \n&=\\int {{3^{1-x^4}}\\cdot x^3 }\\, \\textrm{d}x \\\\[5pt]\n&=  \\int {3^{u}} \\cdot \\left(-\\dfrac{1}{4}\\right) \\textrm{d}u\\\\[5pt]\n                                                     &= -\\dfrac{1}{4}\\int {3^{u}}  \\textrm{d}u\\\\[5pt]\n                                                     &=  -\\dfrac{1}{4}\\cdot\\dfrac{3^{u}}{\\ln 3} + C\\\\[5pt]\n                                                     &=   -\\dfrac{3^{1-x^4}}{4\\ln 3}+ C.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e8720b44-317d-590e-a251-bfcec97e0513" :knowledge-point/title "Integrating Products Containing Other Exponential Functions"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184351 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186183947 :question/id #uuid "70fd3433-e905-5643-8e55-0bdf774d781e" :question/math-academy-id "e-14404" :question/problem "Calculate  $$\n\\displaystyle \\int \\dfrac {e^{2x}} {e^{2x} + 1} \\, \\textrm{d}x.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that the numerator is proportional to the derivative of the denominator. So, we introduce the new variable $u = e^{2x}+1.$ Differentiating gives \n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}u}{\\textrm{d}x}= 2e^{2x}\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad\n\\dfrac{1}{2}\\, \\textrm{d}u = e^{2x} \\, \\textrm{d}x.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{{e}^{2x}}{{e}^{2x}+1}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{1}{{e}^{2x}+1}\\cdot {e}^{2x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{1}{u}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\int \\frac{1}{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|u|+C \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|{e}^{2x}+1|+C \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{2x}+1)+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that, in the last step, we used the fact that $e^{2x} + 1 > 0$ for all $x.$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ed3b6c3d-b840-50ef-9e6b-584bf14877b3" :knowledge-point/title "More Complex Cases"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184352 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186183948 :question/id #uuid "a99e8d2c-1934-57a9-9a6a-578ec9230f6f" :question/math-academy-id "e-14407" :question/problem "Evaluate the integral $$\n\\displaystyle{\\int_{0}^{\\sqrt {\\ln 4}} 16xe^{-2x^2}\\, \\textrm{d}x}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let's introduce the new variable $u=-2x^2.$ Differentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}u}{\\textrm{d}x}=-4x\n\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad\n-4 \\, \\textrm{d}u=16x \\, \\textrm{d}x.\n$$\n\nNext, we determine the new limits of integration:\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$x$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\sqrt {\\ln 4}$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$u$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-2\\ln 4$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nTherefore, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}}16x{e}^{-2{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}}{e}^{-2{x}^{2}}\\cdot 16x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}{e}^{u}\\cdot (-4\\,\\text{d}u) \\\\  & =-4{\\int }_{0}^{-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}{e}^{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-4{e}^{u}\\,{|}_{0}^{-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡4} \\\\  & =-4({e}^{-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}-{e}^{0}) \\\\  & =-4(\\frac{1}{16}-1) \\\\  & =4\\left(\\frac{15}{16}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{15}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "114e3660-63d9-511d-b89c-e71081ffa1e5" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating Definite Integrals"}] :topic/math-academy-id 3770 :topic/next [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048996} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049202} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049380} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049947} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049952} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050207} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050217} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050379} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050929} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186051015}] :topic/title "Integrating Exponential Functions Using Substitution"}]}
